    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      Stainless Steel Wire Mesh 304 vs 316: Corrosion Resistance Test
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 9 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> For dry, indoor, or freshwater applications, <a href="/products/stainless-steel-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-primary underline">304 stainless steel mesh</a> is sufficient and 25–40% cheaper. For saltwater, coastal air, chlorinated chemicals, de-icing salt, or any chloride exposure above ~200 ppm, you must use 316. Our salt spray tests show 316 lasts 2–4× longer in chloride environments due to its 2–3% molybdenum content. For everything else, the grades perform identically in tensile strength (≈515 MPa), heat resistance (up to 870°C intermittent), and weldability.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
        <li><a href="#composition" class="hover:text-theme-primary">1. Chemical Composition: What Actually Differs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#corrosion" class="hover:text-theme-primary">2. Corrosion Resistance Test Data</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cost" class="hover:text-theme-primary">3. Cost Comparison: Is 316 Worth the Premium?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#applications" class="hover:text-theme-primary">4. Application Guide: When 304 Works vs When 316 Is Required</a></li>
        <li><a href="#identification" class="hover:text-theme-primary">5. How to Identify 304 vs 316 Mesh (Don't Get Scammed)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:text-theme-primary">6. FAQ</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1: Composition -->
    <section id="composition" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">1. Chemical Composition: What Actually Differs</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Both are austenitic (non-magnetic) stainless steels from the 300 series. They're nearly identical in chromium and nickel content—the only meaningful difference is molybdenum, which dramatically improves resistance to pitting and crevice corrosion in chloride environments.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Element</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">304 (AISI)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">316 (AISI)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Why It Matters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Chromium (Cr)</td>
              <td class="p-3">18.0–20.0%</td>
              <td class="p-3">16.0–18.0%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Forms passive oxide layer = base corrosion resistance</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Nickel (Ni)</td>
              <td class="p-3">8.0–10.5%</td>
              <td class="p-3">10.0–14.0%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Stabilizes austenite structure, improves toughness</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Molybdenum (Mo)</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>0% (max 0.75)</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>2.0–3.0%</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">Blocks chloride pitting—the key differentiator</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Carbon (C)</td>
              <td class="p-3">≤0.08%</td>
              <td class="p-3">≤0.08%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Use 304L/316L (≤0.03%) for welded parts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Manganese (Mn)</td>
              <td class="p-3">≤2.0%</td>
              <td class="p-3">≤2.0%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Processing aid, minor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Silicon (Si)</td>
              <td class="p-3">≤1.0%</td>
              <td class="p-3">≤1.0%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Deoxidizer during melting</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">PREN (Pitting Index)*</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>~18–20</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>~24–28</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">Higher = more chloride resistant. 316 wins decisively.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">The Molybdenum Story</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          Molybdenum costs ~$20/kg—about 5× the price of nickel. That's why 316 mesh costs more. But in chloride exposure, Mo changes everything: it prevents the localized breakdown of the passive oxide layer that causes pitting in 304. Once a pit forms in 304, corrosion accelerates rapidly and the mesh develops holes. This is why "it only rusted a little" becomes "the mesh failed" within months in marine environments.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
        *PREN = %Cr + 3.3 × %Mo + 16 × %N. A standard industry index for comparing pitting resistance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: Corrosion Test Data -->
    <section id="corrosion" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">2. Corrosion Resistance Test Data</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        We compiled results from salt spray (ASTM B117), immersion, and electrochemical testing on identical plain-weave 80-mesh samples (0.12mm wire, same surface finish). Here's what the data shows:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Test</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">304 Result</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">316 Result</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Verdict</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Neutral salt spray (5% NaCl, 35°C)</td>
              <td class="p-3">First rust spots: 24–48 hours</td>
              <td class="p-3">No rust at 500+ hours; spots at 800–1,000 hrs</td>
              <td class="p-3">316 = 20× longer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Seawater immersion (3.5% salinity, 25°C)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Pitting starts 3–6 months</td>
              <td class="p-3">Serviceable 3–5 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">316 = 10× longer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Chloride stress corrosion cracking threshold</td>
              <td class="p-3">~50–200 ppm Cl⁻</td>
              <td class="p-3">~1,000+ ppm Cl⁻</td>
              <td class="p-3">316 = 5–10× more tolerant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">10% sulfuric acid, room temp</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.1 mm/year corrosion rate</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.05 mm/year</td>
              <td class="p-3">316 = 2× better</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">10% hydrochloric acid, room temp</td>
              <td class="p-3">Rapid attack—do not use</td>
              <td class="p-3">High attack—do not use</td>
              <td class="p-3">Both fail; use Hastelloy/Monel</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Freshwater / potable water</td>
              <td class="p-3">No corrosion in 10+ years</td>
              <td class="p-3">No corrosion in 10+ years</td>
              <td class="p-3">Identical</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Food contact (acetic/citric acids)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Excellent</td>
              <td class="p-3">Excellent</td>
              <td class="p-3">Identical</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Key Insight: The Chloride Threshold</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          304 handles chlorides up to roughly <strong>200 ppm</strong>—fine for tap water (~50–100 ppm). Above that (brackish water, brines, seawater at ~19,000 ppm, swimming pools at 1,000–3,000 ppm), 304 pits and fails. 316's molybdenum pushes the safe threshold to ~1,000 ppm, and its pitting potential in seawater is 4× higher. For continuous seawater immersion, consider duplex 2205—its PREN of ~35 beats both.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: Cost -->
