              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">304 Price (USD/m²)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">316 Price (USD/m²)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">316 Premium</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">Standard roll (30m)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$8–12</td>
              <td class="p-3">$12–18</td>
              <td class="p-3">+40–50%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">Fine mesh (200+)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$15–25</td>
              <td class="p-3">$25–40</td>
              <td class="p-3">+50–60%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3">Dutch weave</td>
              <td class="p-3">$40–80</td>
              <td class="p-3">$60–120</td>
              <td class="p-3">+40–50%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">The Honest Math</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          A 316 mesh that costs 50% more but lasts 10× longer in seawater is dramatically cheaper per year of service. But in a dry warehouse or freshwater plant, 316's premium buys you nothing—the mesh will outlast the equipment either way. <strong>Spend the premium only where chlorides justify it.</strong> Beware suppliers quoting "316" at 304 prices: at current molybdenum costs, that's a red flag for substitution.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: Application Guide -->
    <section id="applications" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">4. Application Guide: When 304 Works vs When 316 Is Required</h2>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">✓ Choose 304 When:</h3>
          <ul class="text-theme-secondary text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>• Indoor architectural screens & decorative panels</li>
            <li>• Freshwater filtration, irrigation, municipal water</li>
            <li>• Food processing (non-salty: flour, sugar, grains)</li>
            <li>• General industrial filtration (solvents, oils, air)</li>
            <li>• Screen printing (99% of industry standard)</li>
            <li>• Agricultural & greenhouse use (away from coast)</li>
            <li>• Budget is tight and environment is controlled</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">⚠ Must Use 316 When:</h3>
          <ul class="text-theme-secondary text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>• Marine/coastal: boat intake screens, dock enclosures</li>
            <li>• Swimming pool & spa equipment (chlorinated water)</li>
            <li>• Chemical processing with chlorides or mild acids</li>
            <li>• Pharmaceutical & medical (regulatory preference)</li>
            <li>• Coastal architecture (within 5 km of saltwater)</li>
            <li>• De-icing salt exposure (roads, bridges, tunnels)</li>
            <li>• Any chloride level >200 ppm in the process fluid</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        For mesh sizing in either grade, use our <a href="/charts/mesh-size-chart/" class="text-theme-primary underline">interactive mesh size chart</a> to match your filtration requirements. Both grades are available across the full range from coarse 4 mesh to ultra-fine 635 mesh.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Temperature Considerations</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          Both grades share the same continuous service limit of ~870°C (1,600°F) in oxidizing atmospheres and ~925°C intermittent. At elevated temperatures, 304 actually holds slightly better oxidation resistance in clean air. However, in boiler and flue-gas environments with sulfur compounds, 316's higher nickel content gives it an edge. For sustained service above 500°C, avoid welded assemblies in either grade unless you specify the low-carbon L versions (304L/316L) to prevent sensitization and intergranular corrosion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5: Identification -->
    <section id="identification" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">5. How to Identify 304 vs 316 Mesh (Don't Get Scammed)</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Unscrupulous suppliers sometimes substitute 201 or low-grade 304 for ordered 316—the price difference is pure profit for them and catastrophic failure for you. Here's how to verify what you received:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Method</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Cost</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Accuracy</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">XRF analyzer (PMI test)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$200–500 per lot</td>
              <td class="p-3">99%+; reads Mo % directly</td>
              <td class="p-3">Gold standard. Hire SGS/BV for big orders.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Molybdenum spot test (chemical)</td>
              <td class="p-3">~$20 kit</td>
              <td class="p-3">Good; detects Mo presence</td>
              <td class="p-3">316 turns dark/color reaction; 304 stays unchanged</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Spark test (grinder)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Free</td>
              <td class="p-3">Rough; differentiates SS from carbon steel only</td>
              <td class="p-3">Won't separate 304 from 316</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Magnet test</td>
              <td class="p-3">Free</td>
              <td class="p-3">Low; only catches 201/fake SS</td>
              <td class="p-3">Both 304 & 316 are non-magnetic when annealed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Mill certificate review</td>
              <td class="p-3">Free</td>
              <td class="p-3">High if verified</td>
              <td class="p-3">Check heat numbers against mill; certs can be forged</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Buying Protection Checklist</h3>
        <ul class="text-theme-secondary space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li>✓ Specify grade in PO with ASTM standard (ASTM A580 for wire)</li>
          <li>✓ Require mill certificate from raw material supplier (not just mesh maker)</li>
          <li>✓ For orders >$5,000, budget for third-party PMI testing</li>
          <li>✓ Do a molybdenum spot test on arrival—takes 5 minutes</li>
          <li>✓ Reject any quote where "316" costs the same as 304</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">6. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Will 304 stainless steel mesh rust?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes, eventually—if exposed to chlorides, salt air, or standing contaminated water. "Stainless" means stains less, not never. In dry or freshwater environments, 304 can last decades without visible rust. In coastal areas, you'll see surface rust within weeks to months. Once rust spots appear, pitting follows.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is 316 mesh stronger than 304?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            No. Both have nearly identical tensile strength (~515 MPa minimum for annealed wire), yield strength (~205 MPa), and hardness. 316's advantage is purely corrosion resistance, not mechanical properties. For higher strength, choose thicker wire, twill weave, or a higher-tensile grade like 304H.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What about 316L vs 316?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            316L has lower carbon (≤0.03% vs ≤0.08%), preventing carbide precipitation at weld heat-affected zones. If you're welding mesh into frames or assemblies, specify 316L. For woven mesh used as-is, standard 316 is fine and slightly cheaper.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I use 316 mesh for hydrochloric or sulfuric acid?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Not for HCl—both grades corrode rapidly in hydrochloric acid. For dilute sulfuric at room temperature, 316 handles it (0.05 mm/year); 304 is marginal. For concentrated acids or elevated temperatures, step up to Hastelloy C-276 or Monel 400.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How much longer will 316 last in a coastal environment?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            In marine atmosphere (not immersion), expect 304 to show rust in 6–18 months and develop pitting holes in 2–5 years. 316 typically lasts 10–15+ years in the same conditions. For full seawater immersion, 316 gives 3–5 years; consider duplex 2205 for 15+ year service.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

